  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Cold front arrives today bringing gusty winds to northwest\n   Montana and hazardous, choppy conditions on Flathead Lake.\n\n- Cutoff low this weekend brings rain and possibly some snow\n  showers to northwest Montana and Glacier National Park. \n\nSatellite and radar imagery early this morning place the\napproaching cold front along the Cascade crest. The front will\npush eastward across the Northern Rockies today, driven by an\nupper-level cutoff low migrating into the region through Saturday. \n\nHigh-resolution ensemble guidance (HREF) remains steady regarding\nwind impacts behind the frontal boundary today. Strongest surface\ngusts remain focused along a corridor from Kila and Happy's Inn\nsouth toward Hot Springs, where gusts over 40 mph are expected.  \nWind will also cause choppy, hazardous conditions on Flathead Lake\nwhere the Lake Wind Advisory remains in effect.\n\nMoisture tap with this system is relatively modest. The bulk of\nprecipitation will set up across Clearwater County, Idaho up\nthrough northwest Montana, with storm total rainfall generally\nremaining under 0.50 inches. Cold air filtering in behind the\nfront will drag freezing levels down over the weekend; latest \nguidance maintains roughly a 30 percent chance of snow at Logan \nPass. While snow accumulations will be light and elevation-\ndependent, raw, cold, and wet conditions in the Glacier National \nPark backcountry will pose hypothermia risks for outdoor \nrecreationists.\n\nAs the upper low departs, the Northern Rockies will settle into a\nwesterly flow regime through mid-week. Model guidance is in good \nagreement that a secondary low pressure system will track well to \nour south, moving across California and the Great Basin into the \nCentral Plains, keeping weather across western Montana and north-\ncentral Idaho relatively benign through Thursday.\n\nBy late next week and into next weekend, long-range ensembles are\nbeginning to trend toward a deeper upper-level trough impacting\nthe Northern Rockies. If this trend holds, it will bring a return\nof cooler temperatures and increased shower activity to start the\nfirst weekend of October.",
